>
>... one huge room
>in the center with all kinds of monsters, and one little one on each 
>side with the stairs.  The problem was that the large number of beasts
>made the game unbearably slow. ...
>Also, as it stands every hit you
>see is presented as a --more-- message: the presentation of each step
>in the battle will be like a chapter of War and Peace.  One line at
>a time.
>

This is what nethack's current algorithm does when there are lots of
monsters visible.  It's usually nice, but it seems unrealistic,
considering how perception is affected by information overload, lack
of detail in peripheral vision, and inability to see through things.

>One way to get partly around this would be to make the battlefield
>dark, or murky, or smoky, or otherwise kind of opaque, and then
>have the game only move the beasts near you.  But that wouldn't
>look so impressive.

What if they all move but you only get detailed information on
the most noticeable ones?
